[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Plans and git refs

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[NARRATIVE]
UID: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-PLANS-IN-REFS
TITLE: Plans and git refs
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
WIDGET: rows
STATEMENT: >>>
Which ref the canon lives on, the long-lived branch, squash merges, and the missing merge joint.

- Only a lean exists: plans may not belong on main; commit only the snapshot that led to work. No ref, tag or branch is named. See [LINK: DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MEMBERSHIP-AND-COLLECTION-CONTRADICT]. [OPEN: DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN]
- The counter-argument: the canon cannot query git, and its intended reader has no memory of yesterday. See [LINK: DEC-SYSTEM-PURPOSE]. [WORKS]
- Canon means committed, reviewed and ratified by merge — yet origin/main holds zero .sdoc files. Which ref the canon lives on is never said. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MERGE-BOUNDARY-IS-NOT-A-JOINT] and [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MEMBERSHIP-AND-COLLECTION-CONTRADICT]. [GAP]
- Work accumulates on one long-lived branch with no pull request during development. At a milestone it is cut into pull requests, each cut bounded by atomicity; acceptance happens at that second review. See [LINK: DEC-LONG-LIVED-BRANCH]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]. [WORKS]
- A squash merge keeps the tree, so hashes stay valid, but destroys per-commit attribution. The pull request is the durable unit, and the fail-safe is running the fingerprint check on the tree about to become main. See [LINK: MECH-FP-MAIN-LINEAGE]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-MECHANISM]. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MERGE-BOUNDARY-IS-NOT-A-JOINT]. [WORKS]
- There is no joint before a merge: merge and rewrite fire only after the fact, the pre-push joint can be bypassed, and CI is not a joint. Every merge-time rule has nowhere to attach. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MERGE-BOUNDARY-IS-NOT-A-JOINT]. [GAP]
<<<
